<section class=3D"gb-all-page" id=3D"about-support" aria-labelledby=3D"abou=
t-support-title"><h1 id=3D"about-support-title">Support</h1><p class=3D"gb-=
lede">Version 3.0 targets Baseline widely available: the web platform featu=
res it uses have worked in all major browsers for at least 30 months.</p><p=
 class=3D"gb-all-source">Online: <a href=3D"https://material-web.dev/3.0/ab=
out/support/">https://material-web.dev/3.0/about/support/</a></p><div class=
=3D"gb-content"><div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"about-support--browsers" tabindex=3D"-1">Browsers</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#about-support--browsers"><span class=3D"offscr=
een">Link to =E2=80=9CBrowsers=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#=
</span></a></div>
<p>Version 3.0 targets <strong>Baseline widely available</strong>: the web =
platform features it
uses have worked in all major browsers for at least 30 months. Newer featur=
es
(such as <code>corner-shape</code> or <code>light-dark()</code>) are only u=
sed behind <code>@supports</code>
guards or with a fallback, so components stay usable in older engines. The
repository checks this with <code>npm run gb:baseline</code>. See
<a href=3D"#about-browser-support">Browser support</a> for the minimum vers=
ions.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"about-support--faq" tabindex=3D"-1">FAQ</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#about-support--faq"><span class=3D"offscreen">=
Link to =E2=80=9CFAQ=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a=
></div>
<div class=3D"heading h3">
<h3 id=3D"about-support--how-do-i-change-the-color-of-a-button" tabindex=3D=
"-1">How do I change the color of a button?</h3>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#about-support--how-do-i-change-the-color-of-a-=
button"><span class=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CHow do I change the colo=
r of a button?=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div=
>
<p>Components read <code>--md-sys-color-*</code> tokens. Override the key c=
olor a component
uses on the element or a class. Buttons use <code>primary</code>, and the s=
tates (hover,
focus, pressed) are derived from it.</p>
