<section class=3D"gb-all-page" id=3D"about-size" aria-labelledby=3D"about-s=
ize-title"><h1 id=3D"about-size-title">Bundle Sizes</h1><p class=3D"gb-lede=
">Every md-gb- component is checked against a Brotli-compressed size budget=
 for its JavaScript and CSS (the measured size plus 10% headroom), defined =
in scripts/size/gb-budgets.json.</p><p class=3D"gb-all-source">Online: <a h=
ref=3D"https://material-web.dev/3.0/about/size/">https://material-web.dev/3=
.0/about/size/</a></p><div class=3D"gb-content"><p>Every <code>md-gb-*</cod=
e> component is checked against a Brotli-compressed size budget
for its JavaScript and CSS (the measured size plus 10% headroom), defined i=
n
<code>scripts/size/gb-budgets.json</code>. For example, <code>md-gb-button<=
/code> stays within about
4.8 KB of JavaScript and 1.5 KB of CSS, and <code>md-gb-badge</code> within=
 about 1 KB and
0.5 KB. Import only the components you use to keep your bundle small.</p>
</div></section>
<section class=3D"gb-all-page" id=3D"about-browser-support" aria-labelledby=
=3D"about-browser-support-title"><h1 id=3D"about-browser-support-title">Bro=
wser support</h1><p class=3D"gb-lede">Version 3.0 only uses web platform fe=
atures that are Baseline widely available.</p><p class=3D"gb-all-source">On=
line: <a href=3D"https://material-web.dev/3.0/about/browser-support/">https=
://material-web.dev/3.0/about/browser-support/</a></p><div class=3D"gb-cont=
ent"><div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"about-browser-support--targets" tabindex=3D"-1">Targets</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#about-browser-support--targets"><span class=3D=
"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CTargets=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"t=
rue">#</span></a></div>
<p>Version 3.0 only uses web platform features that are Baseline widely ava=
ilable.
The oldest browsers the build checks against are:</p>
<div class=3D"gb-table" tabindex=3D"0" role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Table =
7"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Browser</th>
<th>Minimum version</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Chrome</td>
<td>124</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Edge</td>
<td>124</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Firefox</td>
<td>125</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Safari</td>
<td>17.4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>iOS Safari</td>
<td>17.4</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><code>node scripts/gb-css-validate.mjs</code> prints these values, and t=
he CSS is compiled
against them. They move as Baseline moves, so rerun the script to see the
current numbers.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"about-browser-support--features-behind-a-fallback" tabindex=3D"-1=
">Features behind a fallback</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#about-browser-support--features-behind-a-fallb=
ack"><span class=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CFeatures behind a fallback=
=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>Newer features are either guarded with <code>@supports</code> or allowed=
 to be ignored
where a browser lacks them. The exceptions are listed, with the reason for =
each,
in <code>scripts/gb-baseline-allow.json</code>. In short:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>field-sizing</code>: a textarea keeps its fixed rows and scrolls.=
</li>
<li><code>interpolate-size</code>: size changes to intrinsic keywords jump =
instead of
animating.</li>
<li><code>starting-style</code>: an element appears without its enter trans=
ition.</li>
<li><code>transition-behavior</code>: discrete properties change at once.</=
li>
<li><code>scrollbar-gutter</code>: no reserved gutter, so a small layout sh=
ift when a
scrollbar appears.</li>
<li><code>overscroll-behavior</code>: scroll chaining at the edge is not co=
ntained.</li>
<li><code>view-transitions</code>: the view transition styles never match.<=
/li>
<li><code>container-scroll-state-queries</code>: the app bar does not switc=
h to its stuck
look.</li>
<li><code>forced-colors</code>: ignored where forced colors mode does not e=
xist (Safari).</li>
</ul>
<p>Guard your own newer CSS the same way, so older browsers keep a usable
fallback:</p>
