<section class=3D"gb-all-page" id=3D"styles-color" aria-labelledby=3D"style=
s-color-title"><h1 id=3D"styles-color-title">Color</h1><p class=3D"gb-lede"=
>Color creates meaning and communicates hierarchy, state, and brand.</p><p =
class=3D"gb-all-source">Online: <a href=3D"https://material-web.dev/3.0/sty=
les/color/">https://material-web.dev/3.0/styles/color/</a></p><div class=3D=
"gb-content"><div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--color-scheme" tabindex=3D"-1">Color scheme</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--color-scheme"><span class=3D"off=
screen">Link to =E2=80=9CColor scheme=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"=
true">#</span></a></div>
<p>The page follows the subpages of the
<a href=3D"https://m3.material.io/styles/color/overview">M3 color guidance<=
/a> in order. All
imports come from <code>@material/web/labs/gb/styles/color/</code>.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--color-system" tabindex=3D"-1">Color system</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--color-system"><span class=3D"off=
screen">Link to =E2=80=9CColor system=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"=
true">#</span></a></div>
<p>The system turns a few inputs into a complete, accessible set of colors.=
 Dynamic
color starts from a source color, runs it through a scheme algorithm, deriv=
es
five key colors (primary, secondary, tertiary, neutral, neutral variant) pl=
us
error, builds a tonal palette for each in HCT (hue, chroma, tone), and assi=
gns
tones to color roles. Roles come with a pairing rule: a color and its <code=
>on-</code>
color are chosen so text and icons stay legible. Each role has three contra=
st
levels (standard, medium, high), so the same scheme can serve people who ne=
ed
more contrast.</p>
<p><img src=3D"/theming/images/color-scheme-light.webp" alt=3D"Full palette=
 derived from baseline colors" title=3D"From five key colors, roles are aut=
omatically assigned that map to light theme components"></p>
<p><img src=3D"/theming/images/color-scheme-dark.webp" alt=3D"Dark palette =
derived from baseline colors" title=3D"From five key colors, roles are auto=
matically assigned that map to dark theme components"></p>
<p>In labs/gb:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>md-color-tokens.css</code> is the static baseline: every role as =
a
<code>--md-sys-color-&lt;role&gt;</code> custom property, with light and da=
rk values.</li>
<li><code>md-color-tokens-contrast.css</code> adds medium and high contrast=
. High contrast
follows <code>prefers-contrast: more</code>, and <code>.md-contrast-standar=
d</code>,
<code>.md-contrast-medium</code> and <code>.md-contrast-high</code> select =
a level for a subtree.</li>
<li><code>md-color-tokens-full.css</code> also declares the reference palet=
te
(<code>--md-ref-palette-*</code>), the tonal palettes the roles are picked =
from.</li>
<li><code>dynamic-color.ts</code> runs the generation steps at runtime with=
 Material Color
Utilities (see Dynamic color below).</li>
</ul>
<p>The new tone-based surface roles (container levels, dim, and bright) and=
 the
fixed accent roles from the latest spec are all part of the tokens, so noth=
ing
extra is needed to use them. HCT is also available as a small API (see
Advanced customization).</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--color-roles" tabindex=3D"-1">Color roles</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--color-roles"><span class=3D"offs=
creen">Link to =E2=80=9CColor roles=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"tr=
ue">#</span></a></div>
<p>A role is a named job for a color, not a specific hue. Components read r=
oles
(<code>primary</code>, <code>surface-container</code>) instead of hex value=
s, so a new scheme
re-themes everything. The spec groups them as accent roles (primary,
secondary, tertiary, error, each with <code>on-</code>, <code>-container</c=
ode> and <code>on-container</code>
variants), surface roles with <code>on-surface</code> and <code>on-surface-=
variant</code>, outline
roles, inverse roles, and add-on roles: fixed accents and bright and dim
surfaces. Layer from lower to higher emphasis, and always put <code>on-</co=
de> content on
its matching background.</p>
<div class=3D"gb-table" tabindex=3D"0" role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Table =
24"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Role</th>
<th>Tokens</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Primary, secondary, tertiary</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-&lt;accent&gt;</code>, <code>-on-&lt;accent&gt;</c=
ode>, <code>-&lt;accent&gt;-container</code>, <code>-on-&lt;accent&gt;-cont=
ainer</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Fixed accents</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-&lt;accent&gt;-fixed</code>, <code>-&lt;accent&gt;=
-fixed-dim</code>, <code>-on-&lt;accent&gt;-fixed</code>, <code>-on-&lt;acc=
ent&gt;-fixed-variant</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Error</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-error</code>, <code>-on-error</code>, <code>-error=
-container</code>, <code>-on-error-container</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Surface</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-surface</code>, <code>-surface-dim</code>, <code>-=
surface-bright</code>, <code>-surface-container-lowest</code> through <code=
>-surface-container-highest</code>, <code>-on-surface</code>, <code>-on-sur=
face-variant</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Outline</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-outline</code>, <code>-outline-variant</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inverse</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-inverse-surface</code>, <code>-inverse-on-surface<=
/code>, <code>-inverse-primary</code></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Other</td>
<td><code>--md-sys-color-scrim</code>, <code>-shadow</code></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Fixed accents keep the same value in light and dark themes, for content =
that
should not flip, such as a brand surface. Use <code>surface-dim</code> and
<code>surface-bright</code> for areas that stay darker or brighter than the=
 main surface
in both themes.</p>
