<p>The deprecated <code>background</code> and <code>surface-tint</code> rol=
es are not defined; use
<code>surface</code> and a surface container instead.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--choosing-a-scheme" tabindex=3D"-1">Choosing a schem=
e</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--choosing-a-scheme"><span class=
=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CChoosing a scheme=E2=80=9D</span> <span ari=
a-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>Pick <strong>static</strong> color when you want a fixed, predictable pa=
lette: a brand
identity, a product with a strict visual identity, or a small, controlled U=
I.
Pick <strong>dynamic</strong> color when the palette should come from the p=
erson or the
content: a wallpaper, a profile color, an album cover. You can also mix
both: a dynamic scheme for most of the UI and static colors for brand and
semantic meaning.</p>
<p>In labs/gb that is a choice of which stylesheet or function you load:</p=
>
<ul>
<li>Static: import <code>md-color-tokens.css</code> (and <code>md-color-tok=
ens-contrast.css</code>
if you want contrast levels). No JavaScript.</li>
<li>Dynamic: call <code>themeFromSource()</code>, <code>themeFromImage()</c=
ode> or <code>applyTheme()</code>,
which output the same custom properties and override the static ones.</li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--static-color" tabindex=3D"-1">Static color</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--static-color"><span class=3D"off=
screen">Link to =E2=80=9CStatic color=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"=
true">#</span></a></div>
<p>The spec has two kinds. <strong>Baseline</strong> is the default purple =
scheme, ready to use
and a good starting point for prototypes. <strong>Custom brand</strong> kee=
ps the same
roles but replaces the key colors with your brand colors, usually exported =
from
the Material Theme Builder or a design tool.</p>
<p>Baseline in labs/gb is the default stylesheet. It is also part of <code>=
m3Styles</code>,
which <code>adoptStyles()</code> loads.</p>
