<p>To get the theme without applying it, use <code>themeFromSource()</code>=
 or
<code>themeFromImage(img)</code>, and <code>themeToStyleSheet(theme)</code>=
 for a stylesheet.</p>
<ul>
<li>Variants: <code>tonal-spot</code>, <code>neutral</code>, <code>vibrant<=
/code>, <code>expressive</code>, <code>fidelity</code>,
<code>content</code>, <code>monochrome</code>, <code>rainbow</code>, and <c=
ode>fruit-salad</code>. The spec page does
not define these variants or any version of their rules; they come from
Material Color Utilities, which has 2025 rules only for tonal spot,
neutral, vibrant and expressive. The others use the original rules, so
labs/gb does too. <code>theme.specVersion</code> reports what was used.</li=
>
<li>Contrast: <code>standard</code>, <code>medium</code>, <code>high</code>=
, or a number from -1 to 1.
<code>applyTheme()</code> accepts <code>contrast: 'auto'</code>, which uses=
 high contrast when
the user prefers more contrast.</li>
<li>Platform: <code>phone</code> (also right for the web) or <code>watch</c=
ode>.</li>
<li><code>applyTheme(target, ...)</code> accepts a document, a shadow root,=
 or an element.
An element gets inline custom properties, so its subtree has its own
scheme. <code>clearTheme()</code> removes it.</li>
<li>Android's system-wide wallpaper source and the Material Theme Builder
preview are not part of labs/gb; the library only generates the scheme.</li=
>
</ul>
<p>When using content-based color, keep semantic colors (error, success) st=
able
and make sure the surrounding UI stays readable if the image is very
saturated or very dark. The scheme already guarantees accessible on-color
pairs.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-color--advanced-customization" tabindex=3D"-1">Advanced cu=
stomization</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-color--advanced-customization"><span cl=
ass=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CAdvanced customization=E2=80=9D</span> <=
span aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>The spec lists three kinds of customization. This table shows what labs/=
gb
covers.</p>
<div class=3D"gb-table" tabindex=3D"0" role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Table =
25"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Spec topic</th>
<th>In labs/gb</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Combine multiple color schemes</td>
<td>Yes: call <code>applyTheme(element, ...)</code> on different elements, =
each with its own source, variant and contrast.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Map or remap colors on UI elements</td>
<td>Yes: components read roles, so reassign a role in CSS (<code>--md-sys-c=
olor-surface: var(--md-sys-color-surface-container);</code>) on a subtree.<=
/td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Define static colors</td>
<td>Yes: <code>customColors</code> adds a color with four roles.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Define custom color roles</td>
<td>Yes: <code>customRoles</code> takes a name, a palette and a tone for li=
ght and dark (optionally per contrast level).</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Define your own baseline scheme</td>
<td>Yes: override the static tokens, or pass <code>palettes</code>.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Define your own dynamic scheme</td>
<td>Yes: <code>palettes</code> takes your own primary, secondary, tertiary,=
 neutral, neutral variant and error key colors.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Use color fidelity</td>
<td>Yes: <code>fidelity</code> per role.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Harmonize colors</td>
<td>Yes: <code>customColors</code> harmonize toward the source by default, =
and <code>harmonize(color, source)</code> is exported.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
