<section class=3D"gb-all-page" id=3D"styles-elevation" aria-labelledby=3D"s=
tyles-elevation-title"><h1 id=3D"styles-elevation-title">Elevation</h1><p c=
lass=3D"gb-lede">Elevation is the distance between two surfaces on the z-ax=
is.</p><p class=3D"gb-all-source">Online: <a href=3D"https://material-web.d=
ev/3.0/styles/elevation/">https://material-web.dev/3.0/styles/elevation/</a=
></p><div class=3D"gb-content"><p>Elevation is the distance between two sur=
faces on the z-axis. It tells people
which surface sits in front, which one sits behind, and which one is the
current focus. Every surface and component has an elevation, so keep the
number of levels small.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-elevation--levels" tabindex=3D"-1">Levels</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-elevation--levels"><span class=3D"offsc=
reen">Link to =E2=80=9CLevels=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#<=
/span></a></div>
<p>There are six levels, 0 to 5. Each one has a token:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>--md-sys-elevation-shadow-0</code> (no shadow) to <code>--md-sys-=
elevation-shadow-5</code></li>
</ul>
<p>Levels 0 to 3 are for resting components. Levels 4 and 5 are for interac=
tion
states, such as hover and drag. Components raise by one level when hovered =
or
focused: a FAB goes from level 3 to level 4, and a filled button goes from
level 0 to level 1.</p>
<p>The tokens are defined in <code>labs/gb/styles/elevation/md-elevation-to=
kens.scss</code>.
Shadow colors come from <code>--md-sys-color-shadow</code>, so they adapt t=
o the color
scheme.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-elevation--resting-levels" tabindex=3D"-1">Resting levels<=
/h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-elevation--resting-levels"><span class=
=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CResting levels=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-h=
idden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>Each component has a fixed resting level. Do not change it on your own.<=
/p>
<div class=3D"gb-table" tabindex=3D"0" role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Table =
29"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Components</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>0</td>
<td>Buttons (filled, tonal, outlined), button group, split button, icon but=
tons, chips, segmented button, cards (filled, outlined), carousel, list, na=
vigation rail, tabs, slider, app bar (not scrolled), full-screen dialog, do=
cked side sheet</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>Elevated button, elevated card, elevated chip, modal navigation drawer,=
 modal bottom sheet, modal side sheet</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>Menu, navigation bar, rich tooltip</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>FAB (small, standard and extended), FAB menu close button, modal dialog=
, date picker, time picker, floating toolbar, full-screen dialog above comp=
act windows (where it is a basic dialog)</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The search bar also rests at level 0 without a shadow, but it shows its =
level
by tone (see below).</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-elevation--showing-elevation-with-tone" tabindex=3D"-1">Sh=
owing elevation with tone</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-elevation--showing-elevation-with-tone"=
><span class=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CShowing elevation with tone=E2=
=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>Elevation can be shown with a tonal surface color instead of a shadow. T=
wo
components do this in gb:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Search bar</strong>: no shadow. Its container uses <code>surfac=
e-container-high</code>, the
tone for level 3.</li>
<li><strong>App bar when scrolled</strong>: no drop shadow. Its container c=
hanges from
<code>surface</code> to <code>surface-container</code>, the tone for level =
2, so it separates from
the content under it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Use tone when a surface only needs to be told apart from what is behind =
it.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-elevation--shadows" tabindex=3D"-1">Shadows</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-elevation--shadows"><span class=3D"offs=
creen">Link to =E2=80=9CShadows=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">=
#</span></a></div>
<p>Shadows are for two cases only: to protect an element on a busy or patte=
rned
background, and to encourage interaction, such as a card that lifts on hove=
r.
Otherwise rely on the tonal difference between surfaces, which is the defau=
lt.
A small, sharp shadow means the surface is close to the one behind it. A la=
rger,
softer shadow means more distance.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-elevation--code-sample" tabindex=3D"-1">Code sample</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-elevation--code-sample"><span class=3D"=
offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CCode sample=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=
=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<figure class=3D"gb-demo" aria-label=3D"A note card that lifts from level 1=
