<div class=3D"gb-table" tabindex=3D"0" role=3D"region" aria-label=3D"Table =
31"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Spring</th>
<th>Expressive</th>
<th>Standard</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Spatial fast / default / slow</td>
<td>350 / 420 / 570ms</td>
<td>160 / 220 / 340ms</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Effects fast / default / slow</td>
<td>140 / 220 / 300ms</td>
<td>140 / 220 / 300ms</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>Springs handle interruption and retargeting well, so prefer them. For
animations without interruptions or gestures you can use the curve fallback=
:
<code>--md-sys-motion-curve-&lt;speed&gt;-&lt;spatial|effects&gt;</code> (a=
 <code>cubic-bezier()</code>) with
<code>--md-sys-motion-curve-&lt;speed&gt;-&lt;spatial|effects&gt;-duration<=
/code>. They carry the
values from the spec's web conversion table.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h3">
<h3 id=3D"styles-motion--reduced-motion" tabindex=3D"-1">Reduced motion</h3=
>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-motion--reduced-motion"><span class=3D"=
offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CReduced motion=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidde=
n=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>Reduced motion is on when the platform asks for it
(<code>prefers-reduced-motion: reduce</code>) or inside an element with the
<code>md-motion-reduced</code> class (spatial motion is off there, fades st=
ay). The spatial springs then become instant (0ms) and
the effects springs keep their timing. People still see fades and color
changes, but not sliding, scaling, parallax or shape morphing.</p>
<p>Do not build your own motion gate. Read the durations from the tokens so=
 the
scheme and reduced motion apply without extra code.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h3">
<h3 id=3D"styles-motion--gestures" tabindex=3D"-1">Gestures</h3>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-motion--gestures"><span class=3D"offscr=
een">Link to =E2=80=9CGestures=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"true">#=
</span></a></div>
<p>A gesture has to keep its momentum: a fling that lets go at 2000px/s mus=
t not
restart from rest, and a settle that is aimed at a new target half way must
not jump. A CSS transition cannot do either, so <code>labs/gb</code> also h=
as the same
springs as physics, in <code>labs/gb/components/shared/spring.ts</code>. <c=
ode>readSpring()</code>
returns the damping ratio and stiffness of a spring token (Expressive or
Standard, whichever applies to the element), and <code>Spring</code> solves=
 it in closed
form and samples it every animation frame:</p>
