<p>Tokens are <code>--md-sys-motion-easing-&lt;set&gt;[-accelerate|-deceler=
ate]</code> and
<code>--md-sys-motion-duration-&lt;short|medium|long|extra-long&gt;&lt;1-4&=
gt;</code>. Short is
50 to 200ms, medium 250 to 400ms, long 450 to 600ms and extra long 700 to
1000ms, in steps of 50ms (extra long in steps of 100ms).</p>
<p>How to choose:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Easing set.</strong> Emphasized for most transitions, with its =
decelerate and
accelerate variants for entering and exiting. Standard for small, quick,
utility transitions.</li>
<li><strong>Easing type.</strong> Plain for elements that begin and end on =
screen,
decelerate when entering, accelerate when leaving for good. An element that
leaves temporarily (a drawer that comes back) uses the plain curve.</li>
<li><strong>Duration.</strong> Bigger transitions and larger distances take=
 longer. Exits
are shorter than enters, because the user's attention has already moved on.=
</li>
</ul>
<p>Reduced motion does not change these tokens. Gate sliding and scaling yo=
urself
(the transition helpers below do) or use the spring tokens, which follow th=
e
setting.</p>
<p>Not covered: the spec's emphasized easing is a path curve. On the web
<code>--md-sys-motion-easing-emphasized</code> is the single <code>cubic-be=
zier(0.2, 0, 0, 1)</code>
approximation, so it is equal to <code>standard</code>.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h2">
<h2 id=3D"styles-motion--transitions" tabindex=3D"-1">Transitions</h2>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-motion--transitions"><span class=3D"off=
screen">Link to =E2=80=9CTransitions=E2=80=9D</span> <span aria-hidden=3D"t=
rue">#</span></a></div>
<p>A transition is a short animation that connects elements or full-screen =
views
and shows how they relate. The spec defines six patterns and notes that the=
y
still use the easing and duration tokens above, not springs. <code>labs/gb<=
/code>
implements them in <code>styles/motion/transitions/</code> with the View Tr=
ansitions API,
and falls back to the Web Animations API when the browser lacks it (or when=
 you
pass <code>viewTransition: false</code>).</p>
<p>The pattern styles are part of <code>m3Styles</code> (see
<a href=3D"#about-quick-start--import">Quick Start</a>). Adopt them on the =
document,
because <code>::view-transition-*</code> rules only work there. The helpers=
 come from
<code>@material/web/labs/gb/styles/motion/transitions/transitions.js</code>=
.</p>
<p>Every helper takes <code>update</code>, a function that changes the DOM =
to the new state,
and returns a promise that resolves when the motion ends. Common options:
<code>reducedMotion</code>, <code>viewTransition</code>, <code>duration</co=
de> and <code>easing</code>. The timings come
from <code>--md-transition-*</code> custom properties (for example
<code>--md-transition-shared-axis-duration</code>), so you can restyle a pa=
ttern in CSS.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h3">
<h3 id=3D"styles-motion--container-transform" tabindex=3D"-1">Container tra=
nsform</h3>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-motion--container-transform"><span clas=
s=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CContainer transform=E2=80=9D</span> <span =
aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>For an element that becomes a new surface and shows more detail: a card =
that
opens a details page, a FAB that opens a sheet, a search box, a list item.
The container's bounds, corner radius and color morph while its content fad=
es
through (Emphasized, 500ms by default). <code>from</code> and <code>to</cod=
e> can be the same element
or different ones. Swap them to collapse back.</p>
