<p><code>target</code> is the scrolling element or its id (the window by de=
fault), <code>origin</code>
the edge it slides toward. It uses the same scroll rules as the app bar and
navigation bar, so they all behave alike: it reveals at the top and when
scrolled up, hides after a short scroll down, never hides while focus is in=
side
it, and reveals when focus moves into it. A hidden element is <code>inert</=
code>. With
reduced motion it fades instead of sliding. Call <code>destroy()</code> whe=
n the element
goes away. Components that already have <code>scroll-behavior=3D"hide"</cod=
e> do not need
this.</p>
<div class=3D"heading h4">
<h4 id=3D"styles-motion--coplanar-components" tabindex=3D"-1">Coplanar comp=
onents</h4>
<a class=3D"anchor" href=3D"#styles-motion--coplanar-components"><span clas=
s=3D"offscreen">Link to =E2=80=9CCoplanar components=E2=80=9D</span> <span =
aria-hidden=3D"true">#</span></a></div>
<p>A coplanar component sits at the same elevation as the main content (a
standard side sheet), so the content shrinks to make room as it enters and
grows back as it exits. <code>coplanar(element, open, options)</code> anima=
tes the
element's size from zero to its natural size with the enter motion (and bac=
k
with the exit motion), so a flex or grid neighbor resizes with it. It shows=
 the
element when opening and sets <code>hidden</code> when closing ends.</p>
